# TIME(2)

## NAME

time, ftime - get date and time

## SYNOPSIS

```c
#include <sys/types.h>

time_t time(time_t *tloc);

#include <sys/timeb.h>

int ftime(struct timeb *tp);
```

## DESCRIPTION

*Time* returns the time since 00:00:00 GMT, Jan. 1, 1970, measured in seconds.

If *tloc* is nonnull, the return value is also stored in the place to which *tloc* points.

The *ftime* entry fills in a structure pointed to by its argument, as defined by
*<sys/timeb.h>*:

```c
struct timeb {
        time_t  time;
        int     millitm;
        int     timezone;
        int     dstflag;
};
```

**Note:** v7's page pulled this in with a **.so** of the installed header; it is written out
here, there being no /usr/include on this system.

The structure contains the time since the epoch in seconds, up to 1000 milliseconds of
more-precise interval, the local timezone (measured in minutes of time westward from
Greenwich), and a flag that, if nonzero, indicates that Daylight Saving time applies locally
during the appropriate part of the year.

## SEE ALSO

date(1), stime(2), ctime(3)

## BESM-6 NOTES

**time_t is one word,** 41 bits of seconds, so there is no 2038 problem - and no second word
for the gate to carry, which is why stime(2) lost an argument.

**The gate takes no argument at all.** The time comes back in the accumulator and the store
through *tloc* is the stub's own doing, which is why this stub is hand-written rather than
generated. See intro(2).

**Ftime is where the zone comes from,** as it did in v7: localtime(3), *mktime* and
*strftime*'s **%z** and **%Z** all ask it. The kernel owns the westward offset and the daylight
flag, and there is no zoneinfo to read.

## ASSEMBLER

**Note:** v7's page gave the PDP-11 form here - a decimal call number and a `sys name; arg ...`
line. Neither is how a call is made on this machine. The gate is one extracode - **$77
SYS_time, SYS_ftime** - and the C calling convention has already put the arguments where it
reads them: 1..n-1 below r15, the last in the accumulator, the result in the accumulator, the
error number in r14.

**The numbering itself is v7's, unchanged.** The macro is defined in <sys/syscall.h>, which is
the only place a syscall number appears on the user side. See intro(2) for the whole contract.
